+  lock until the command's last descendant exits, including detached children
+  and after the launching arc exits. There is no queue order or role queue.
+  A waiter prints the holding project, change, gate and elapsed hold time.
+  Admission expiry starts no command and records `not-admitted`, not a gate
+  failure. `wait_ms` includes exclusive admission and is separate from `run_ms`.
+  Admission accepts positive durations such as `500ms`, `2.5s`, `2m` or `1h`.
+  Both keys are outside evidence identity: they govern admission, not the check.
+  Either declaration may require a lock; an omitted key cannot remove one.
+  Matching lock names take the shortest admission limit, including the default.
+  Different names conflict between project and operator policy; between target
+  and change they report a divergence and the target's lock stays in force.
+
+  `arc verify --all --event integration|publication` selects the event's
+  gates. Evidence identifies its event, tree, command and environment;
+  a publication pass does not satisfy an integration requirement.
+  Historical verification evidence with no recorded event is integration
+  evidence. An omitted event on a gate declaration applies to both events.
+  `arc publication --target <branch> --profile <profile>` runs the complete
+  publication tier at that branch's head. Fresh and cached publication receipts
+  must satisfy current budgets; publication-only gates are outside the
+  integration total. --skip-green keeps the newest structurally matching
+  receipt and its command timing, refusing a budget failure without executing
+  it again. Publication confirms effective budgets, total rules and sources
+  after checking the content. Repository budgets come from a recorded integration
+  destination or the associated open change's destination, plus the publication
+  tree's additions and tightenings and current operator policy. --change selects
+  the open change owning the publication branch; otherwise a unique matching
+  change is inferred. Without a recorded destination or associated change,
+  repository budget authority is unavailable; operator-only budgets still bind.
+  Push and release tooling can use
+  `--revision <full-commit-id>` to require the exact revision queued by the
+  caller and use its exit status as a local guard. Arc makes no network
+  request and installs no hook implicitly. A failed publication names the integrations since the
+  most recent successful publication pass.
+
+  `[selection] inert = ["**/*.md"]` declares paths that affect no gate.
+  Patterns use `policy::glob_match`, as path policy does: `*` stays within a
+  segment, `**` crosses segments, `**/` includes zero directories, and a
+  trailing `/` includes everything beneath that directory. Patterns are
+  case-sensitive and match repository-relative paths.
+
+  A path affects gates whose `paths` match it. Inert paths affect none. A path
+  no explicit gate pattern names affects every gate. Dangerous policy paths
+  affect every gate, including those beneath inert exclusions. A gate without
+  paths is affected by every non-inert path. Renames check both old and new
+  paths. The inventory includes gates from every profile and event.
+
+  Integration selection needs an earlier pass in the same change, with the
+  same declaration and environment. Project adoption of base passes permits
+  cross-change reuse under `arc guide base-passes`. Commit-bound gates do not select
+  across trees. Arc emits `arc-gate-selection/1` as selector `arc:paths` when
+  paths prove the gate unaffected. It asks a declared script only when the
+  gate remains affected; unnamed and dangerous paths cannot be cleared by a
+  script while path selection is declared. An uncertain delta runs the gate.
+  An unreadable danger declaration affects every path; inspection remains
+  available when append policy cannot be parsed.
+
+  Paths and inert exclusions participate in evidence identity. The complete
+  coverage map and dangerous patterns also participate because they determine
+  unnamed-path fallback. Declaring nonempty coverage settings requires a fresh
+  pass; legacy evidence without identity cannot answer a scoped declaration.
+  A declaration without coverage settings retains its default identity. Target
+  coverage stays in force until an edited declaration lands, as with selector
+  changes. Project and operator inert lists combine; differing paths for the
+  same gate conflict. Scoped run receipts retain identity, and delta receipts
+  retain the acknowledged request, answer and its digest for replay.
+
+  A gate's `selector` reads `arc-gate-delta/1` JSON: `source_tree`,
+  `target_tree`, `paths` and `gates`. Its `arc-gate-selection/1` answer
+  includes `complete: true`, `request` equal to the complete input object,
+  and `gates` naming affected checks. A successful stdin write does not prove
+  consumption. Missing, failed, malformed, incomplete, unknown or mismatched
+  answers run the required checks. Selection applies to integration;
+  publication checks every required gate on a changed tree. Reuse receipts
+  retain the passing source tree, target tree, selector and declarations;
+  they grant no result to another event or environment.
+
+GATE COSTS
+  `arc gates` in text and JSON and `arc gates over` diagnose command costs
+  from receipts. Indicators can overlap; they suggest what to measure and do
+  not establish a cause or alter gate acceptance. Time distributions use at
+  most the latest 20 executed runs matching the current command and declared
+  timeout, in the newest recorded environment. Medians use the lower middle.
+  `gates over` uses the selected overrun's effective declarations, including
+  gates declared only on its change, from any checkout. Without an overrun it
+  uses the invoking checkout's declarations.
+  Each measure reports its sample count. Missing fields are unmeasured:
+  legacy duration_ms never substitutes for command, CPU, wait or preparation.
+
+  Five cost kinds:
+  - waiting: paired wall/CPU medians have wall >= 100ms and wall >= twice CPU.
+    CPU is observed child user plus system time; I/O and sleeps can both wait.
+  - queueing: median wait_ms >= 100ms and >= 10% of median command wall time.
+  - duplication: executions on integrated changes divided by integrated changes
+    exceed one. The numerator matches the current command and timeout across
+    environments; the denominator is every readable integrated change in the
+    ledger, including changes answered entirely by reuse. Reuse, selection,
+    base passes, attestation and not-admitted attempts contribute no executions.
+    Changes without an execution still count in the denominator; no integrations is
+    unmeasured, not a zero-cost claim.
+  - preparation: median prepare_ms >= 100ms and >= 10% of median command wall.
+  - real-work: paired wall/CPU measurements exist and the waiting threshold
+    is not met. This is an indicator of command work, not proof of useful work.
+  Waiting/real-work needs paired fields; queueing and preparation need their
+  own field and wall. Absence never means zero.
+
+  The order of work, also printed by `arc gates over`:
+  1. Get the number first.
+  2. Measure serially.
+  3. Remove waste.
+  4. Rank checks by what they guard and cut from the bottom, never by cost alone.
+  5. Make what matters fit.
+  6. Lower concurrency until nothing fails.
+  7. Stop at a named floor.
+  8. Raise the budget last, within the change's allowance.
+
+  Levers, in order, are guidance arc states and does not automate:
+  1. Use an optimized binary for spawned tests.
+  2. Skip durability syscalls behind an explicit test-only switch.
+  3. Use cheaper fixtures.
+  4. Use events in place of sleeps.
+  5. Increase harness parallelism or remove a shared serialization point.
+  6. Call in process where no process is needed.
+  7. Split a suite into gates by area with `paths`.
+  8. Cut tests on evidence of what each has ever caught, ranked by what it guards.
+  Cost and failures inform review; they do not choose which check to remove.
+
+  A gate may declare timings = "checks.tsv". Its command writes the relative
+  file inside its worktree on each execution. Separate gates use separate files.
+  Arc never deletes it. A file unchanged since the attempt began is stale.
+  The file must be regular, with no final symlink and no parent outside the
+  worktree. Ignore generated timings in Git to keep execution evidence clean.
+  Missing or inaccessible files, stale files, and malformed or oversized input
+  produce an advisory receipt error, never a gate failure.
+
+  The UTF-8 line format is `arc-check-timings/1` on the first line, followed
+  by one check per line: name<TAB>milliseconds<TAB>pass|fail. Names are unique,
+  nonempty, at most 128 UTF-8 bytes, and contain no control characters.
+  Milliseconds are unsigned whole decimal integers. At least one check is
+  required. Outcomes describe observations; the command's exit status decides
+  the gate outcome. For example, with literal tab characters:
+
+    arc-check-timings/1
+    parse-config	12	pass
+    reject-corruption	230	fail
+
+  Input is bounded to 65,536 bytes and 512 check entries. An exceeded limit or
+  any malformed entry discards the whole file with an error. Each run keeps
+  at most 32 checks, failures first, then slowest within each outcome, with
+  name as the tie break. Its receipt names the number dropped. These optional
+  check_timings fields travel with command timing through replay and reuse;
+  absence stays absent and older receipts remain valid. The path stays outside
+  declaration identity and declaration-conflict checks: it observes a command
+  and changes nothing the command checks or what its existing evidence proves.
+
+  Reports aggregate direct executions of the current command and timeout in
+  the newest environment. They show the ten slowest retained check names by
+  median cost, participation count, and changes on which the check failed.
+  A check with no retained failure prints "never failed in N runs, costs M
+  seconds". N counts only runs retaining that check, not reuses or missing
+  observations. This is recorded history, not proof that a check caught nothing
+  or that it can be cut. Dropped checks and invalid or absent timings are
+  counted explicitly. Arc builds no result store, impact planner or diff judge.
+
+GATES TOGETHER
+  A gate declaring concurrent = true may share a batch with other consenting
+  gates. Required-set executions run standalone gates first in name order,
+  then the concurrent set. This includes verify --all, snapshot --verify,
+  done, acceptance, merged-tree verification and both landing forms. Every merged
+  declaration must consent; a stricter standalone declaration wins.
+  Duplicate exclusive lock names among concurrent gates are a declaration
+  error, even before measurement. Locks and admission remain per gate.
+  Landing members sharing a gate must require compatible locks; a lock named
+  by either member and their shortest admission bound apply to its execution.
+
+  `arc gates measure --concurrent` executes every declared concurrent gate
+  together five times on a clean tree, without recording verification evidence.
+  This invocation writes local acceptance and requires a writing identity.
+  At least two gates are required; --runs must be five and --gate is refused.
+  Every command must pass every round, with observed command time, and the
+  worktree and head must remain unchanged. It reports per-command wall, CPU,
+  wait and load, per-command spread, batch wall and physical/logical cores.
+  Budgets judge later verification receipts; measurement acceptance establishes
+  coexistence, not compliance with a budget. Measurement replaces the previous
+  local acceptance before running; a failed measurement leaves no acceptance.
+
+  Acceptance binds every member's complete structural declaration identity and
+  the exact concurrent membership, this store and this host's machine identity.
+  Budget, exclusive and admission values do not change structural identity.
+  Changing a structural identity or membership requires measurement again.
+  The private file under <ledger>/concurrent is local administration, excluded
+  from export and replica exchange. An unavailable host identity or unreadable
+  acceptance disables batching. `arc gates` shows consent, acceptance, date,
+  host and member identities. Acceptance is a measurement, not isolation:
+  ignored state and dependencies omitted from declarations remain assumptions.
+
+  Without exact current acceptance the concurrent gates execute alone in name
+  order and explain the missing measurement. A batch of one is standalone.
+  --gate NAME always runs alone. Reuse and paths selection happen first; if
+  they remove members, only an exactly accepted remaining set may run together.
+  Otherwise the remaining gates run alone. If their standalone budgets exceed
+  the total, execution refuses; --rerun can run the full accepted batch.
+  Verification runs name this scheduler as mode declared; individual batch
+  receipts identify actual concurrent execution. --rerun disables reuse but
+  never bypasses measurement. --parallel remains
+  the separate unqualified preflight probe with unknown cleanliness.
+
+  A batch pass is green only with a retained tested tree, a worktree observed
+  clean before the batch, unchanged worktree and head afterward, and current
+  local acceptance of the batch's identities. Dirty waivers cannot excuse a
+  batch. All members share those boundaries; each retains its own result,
+  command time, CPU, load, timeout, budget judgement, lock and admission wait.
+  Receipts name the acquired exclusive lock and its admission_ms limit.
+  Batch receipts retain batch id, acceptance id, producing store, host and
+  member declaration identities. Authorization entries retain the same
+  acceptance reference. The batch id binds the receipt's compact coordinates.
+  Full declarations, measurement date and five rounds remain in the host-local
+  acceptance record. Replay validates frozen references without loading that
+  record; `arc gates` reports its date and identities.
+  One member's failure does not turn another's result into a failure.
+  status, check, show and explain identify the producing batch.
+
+  A direct clean batch execution may justify a gate raise under the usual
+  newest-execution rule. An eligible batch member may seed a base pass; its
+  witness retains the batch and acceptance identity. Current acceptance is checked
+  before using that witness. Imported batch evidence stays factual and cannot
+  be green on a different store, even after that receiver measures its own
+  batch. A local standalone run or a new local accepted batch supplies evidence.
+  Publication and candidate verification execute their checks alone; publication
+  retains its full checks for gates participating in base passes.
+
+  Per integration profile, total adds standalone budgets and the maximum
+  concurrent budget only when that profile's complete concurrent set exactly
+  matches this store's current acceptance. Otherwise all budgets add. Total
+  raises' declaration_sum_ms uses this same cost across profiles. Authorization
+  snapshots bind consent and the local acceptance identity, so a changed or
+  removed measurement cannot inherit an earlier authorization. Historical
+  replay validates frozen receipts without consulting local measurements.
+  Store format 11 fences readers and writers without these batch semantics.
+
+GATE RAISES
+  `arc gates raise GATE --reason TEXT` records a proposed gate increase.
+  `arc gates raise --total --reason TEXT` raises only the integration total;
+  GATE with --total requests both transitions atomically. A reason says what
+  was tried first. Higher numbers must be committed in .arc/gates.toml at the
+  clean change head.
+
+  Removing a gate is reviewed like any diff and needs no raise record. The
+  change still owes the destination's gate under its destination budget.
+  Keeping a gate while removing only its budget is refused; restore its budget
+  or remove the gate. Removing [budget] total or its table while any gate
+  remains is refused; restore the total or remove every gate. Removing
+  every gate and the table together needs no raise. Lowering a gate
+  budget or the total needs no raise.
+
+  The newest direct execution of that gate at the exact head must pass, be
+  observed clean and stable on that head's tree, match the destination check
+  and current environment, exceed the destination budget and fit the proposed
+  one. Reuse, rewritten execution coordinates, attestation, bypass and measurement
+  output cannot establish this need. Later evidence remains subject to ordinary
+  newest-evidence judgement; a raise never masks a failure.
+
+  The allowed increment is old_budget * raise * min(1, added / raise_per),
+  rounded down to milliseconds with checked integer arithmetic. [budget] raise
+  defaults to "20%" and raise_per to 500; raise_per must be positive. Percentages
+  accept up to four decimal places. JSON budget_rules.raise uses millionths;
+  raise_per uses lines. Destination and operator rules supply the
+  trusted allowance; head rules may tighten it. Repeated requests spend from
+  the same destination budget and final delta, without compounding.
+
+  Counting requires the destination revision to be an ancestor of the head
+  and to contain the change's declared base. Unintegrated prerequisites provide
+  no credit to another member.
+  arc-budget-growth/2 compares those endpoint trees once per subject, with
+  pinned 50% rename/copy detection, unlimited rename candidates, NUL-delimited
+  UTF-8 paths, no external diff or text conversion. Eligible nonblank growth
+  is the sum of logical-file growth minus shrinkage and deletions in the same
+  counted scope, floored at zero. Records retain growing and shrinking files
+  with their before/after counts. Moves between files and file splits earn
+  nothing without net scope growth. Whitespace-only changes, pure moves,
+  copies without growth, binary and non-UTF-8 content,
+  symlinks and gitlinks earn nothing. Binary/custom diff attributes from either
+  committed endpoint exclude a file; isolated Git metadata prevents host attributes
+  or driver configuration from granting credit. Uncertain input refuses a raise.
+  Cross-scope moves earn nothing; modified moves inside scope earn only growth.
+  Overlapping patterns count a file once; intermediate commit churn earns nothing.
+
+  A gate's destination paths bound counting; operator paths may narrow the scope.
+  A gate without paths and the total count every path outside trusted inert.
+  Destination and operator inert exclusions restrict growth and shrinkage.
+  Head paths and inert exclusions may remove growth credit; trusted shrinkage
+  remains a debit. Moving a file out of scope debits its counted source.
+  Selection's unnamed-path fallback grants no extra allowance. [budget] exclude
+  in operator policy names generated/vendor paths, independently of inert and
+  gate selection.
+  A repository's exclude list cannot authorize counting exclusions. Administration
+  of operator policy is trusted authority; raise never edits that file.
+
+  This allowance is a per-change brake, not a cumulative growth bound or a
+  judgement of whether added text is valuable. Operator [budget] total is the
+  fixed ceiling for repeated raises. Doctor and gates warn when budgets have no
+  operator ceiling. Review must still assess padding, relevance and the reason.
+
+  Every raised total needs its own explicit transition and allowance, counting
+  all qualifying files. Its required declaration sum, across every profile and
+  the unqualified case, must exceed the old total and fit the new one and the
+  operator ceiling. A new gate can justify a total-only raise without an old
+  gate exceeding its budget. All other effective total rules remain binding.
+
+  The immutable gate-budget-raised event records change, destination revision,
+  exact head/tree, counting algorithm and rule digest, per-file counts, old/new
+  budgets, allowance, cited direct execution or total declaration sum, and reason.
+  Every head or destination change requires re-establishing the record, including
+  amend, rebase, squash and signature/trailer-only rewrites. No rewrite equivalence
+  is assumed. Current applicability is rederived from retained objects and receiver
+  policy; missing objects or changed counting, execution or policy inputs refuse it.
+
+  Snapshot captures the complete immutable raise set. Appending a record requires
+  a fresh snapshot even at the same Git head, and fresh review or recorded debt
+  under the ordinary policy. Status and check expose budget_raises; show JSON
+  retains budget_raises as facts and adds budget_raise_status. Show's budget review
+  checklist names old/new values, scope, allowance, execution, reason, total and
+  ceiling. A debt-backed raise is evidence with review owed, never reviewed.
+
+  Integrate, accept, both landing forms, candidate selection/promotion and fresh
+  or cached publication refuse an unrecorded or stale declaration increase even
+  when every run fits the old budgets. Each landing member owns its allowance;
+  records and line credit are never pooled. An associated publication change uses
+  its validated record; an arbitrary branch cannot authorize its own increase.
+  After integration the destination declaration supplies the higher ordinary
+  budget for later changes. The record and authorization remain historical facts.
+
+  Export/import and replicas transport raises as factual evidence with cited
+  executions and retained head/destination objects. Receiver constraints decide
+  applicability; a producer's acceptance grants no receiver authority. Historical
+  replay does not consult current budgets. Budget-bearing writes/imports adopt
+  store format 9 before publishing evidence; executables through format 8 refuse.
+  Change bundles require the exporter's store-format capability; import checks
+  the reader's supported store format. Export does not adopt the local ledger.
+BASE PASSES
+  `[selection] base_passes = true` in committed .arc/gates.toml opts a project
+  into cross-change path selection. The destination must already declare it;
+  a change cannot enable it for itself. Without an eligible baseline, gates run.
+  An opted-in documentation change can integrate with zero executions on its
+  final content. This relies on the declared coverage being correct. Status,
+  check, show and explain name each base pass, baseline, original execution
+  time, changed paths, declaration and environment assumptions. `verify --rerun`
+  executes every selected gate. `arc gates` counts base passes beside executions.
+
+  After authoritative integration applies, a gate-baseline-recorded completion
+  snapshot retains the project/replica, successful completion ID/digest, target,
+  installed revision/tree, actual profile and each eligible execution's facts. Publication stays
+  inside the destination transition guard; competing integrations serialize.
+  Missing snapshots after interruption are cache misses. Only clean, stable,
+  locally executed integration passes at the installed tree enter a baseline.
+  Each entry retains its full structural declaration and complete coverage
+  context, execution/run IDs and digests, coordinates, command, timeout,
+  environment identity, binding and timing. Output tails and artifacts stay in
+  the original execution ledger.
+  Reused, bypassed, attested, untimed, over-budget and dirty-waived evidence
+  cannot establish an entry. An empty baseline establishes no gate pass.
+
+  Each gate's first observation may use the newest retained eligible local
+  baseline containing an entry for that gate, in both the actual target's and
+  evaluated head's first-parent history. Different gates may cite different
+  baselines; an empty snapshot does not obscure an older execution. Other gates' observations do not consume that eligibility. Any earlier
+  observation of the same gate, at any head, prevents a baseline answer, including
+  a pass, failure, not-admitted attempt or budget refusal. Concurrent verifiers
+  recheck the gate under its change transition lock before publishing an answer.
+  Selection considers at most eight retained baselines for the target/profile,
+  newest first, and stops at the first eligible baseline holding the gate's
+  entry. That entry must match the declaration, environment and coverage and
+  fit the budget in force. Otherwise the gate executes; an older matching or
+  faster entry is never sought. A gate with no retained entry executes.
+  Each witness proves the complete diff from its chosen baseline to the
+  evaluated tree. Paths and Git validation are shared only for the same subject.
+  Later failures, not-admitted attempts and budget refusals cannot be rescued
+  by a baseline. Rewrites do not advance baseline coordinates. Missing objects,
+  nonancestor or force-moved history, changed declaration/coverage context,
+  different actual profiles/events or mismatching/failed environment probes
+  prevent reuse. Commit-bound gates execute at their required commit.
+  Ancestry walks stop beyond the candidate's parents and at 256 first-parent
+  commits; an inconclusive walk is a cache miss. Verified immutable ancestry
+  facts are shared within the command. Each target/profile retains eight newest
+  baseline ref sets. Older baselines lose their refs and cannot be selected,
+  even while another branch keeps their objects reachable. Existing reuse
+  receipts retain their proof objects independently of this cache. One ref
+  retains the target and each distinct execution revision needs at most one more.
+
+  Coverage is the destination's complete gate map and dangerous patterns.
+  Endpoint-tree diffs disable rename detection and retain NUL-delimited names,
+  including deletions, both rename endpoints, modes, types, symlinks and gitlinks.
+  Unreadable, invalidly encoded or oversized deltas run the gates. Dangerous and
+  unnamed paths affect every gate. In opted-in projects .arc/ is protected even
+  under inert patterns. Declaration and selection-input edits cannot waive their
+  own checks. A declared selector does not grant cross-change reuse.
+
+  A verification-base-reused witness cites a baseline event ID and digest and
+  carries its gate, destination revision/tree, target revision, merge target,
+  complete paths and algorithm version. Replay resolves the ledger's immutable
+  baseline, checks its digest and integration authority, then checks direct clean
+  execution provenance, source/destination coordinates, actual profile/event,
+  declarations, environment and coverage classification. Retained Git objects prove
+  the complete diff, committed adoption and first-parent ancestry. Hashes and
+  asserted path lists alone are insufficient. Replay reads neither current
+  budgets nor the source change's ledger; current acceptance judges original
+  command timing against the budget in force. Export and retention include the
+  cited objects. Standalone transport includes only the cited compact baseline
+  records beside the change history; it embeds no execution output. Imported
+  facts cannot seed local baseline authority; imported
+  reuse requires admitted integration authority for its recorded baseline.
+  Witnesses are bounded to 1 MiB. Store format 10 is stamped before baseline or
+  reuse publication and import; executables supporting formats through 9 refuse
+  reads and writes, including writers opened before adoption. Bundles advertise
+  the writer's store format; an importer with a lower maximum refuses them.
+
+  Stack and composed land may consume base passes against the complete final
+  content and combined obligations. They record one baseline for the installed
+  final revision, never for a member's intermediate revision. Candidate checks
+  and promotion consume no base pass and create no target baseline. Provisional
+  integration may consume canonical evidence while owing staging declarations;
+  it creates no canonical baseline. Accept forces executions. Publication
+  creates no integration baseline.
+
+  Publication executes in full every gate that participated in a base pass in
+  integrations since the last successful publication of this target/profile,
+  including integration-only gates. Integration completion IDs delimit this
+  obligation, so evidence recorded before publication and integrated afterwards
+  remains owed. Participant discovery shares the states already read for
+  publication authority and inspects integrations since the previous audit;
+  it does not reduce change ledgers again. Per-change base-pass receipts name
+  the source declarations, including gates absent from the publication tier.
+  A missing post-integration cache snapshot does not erase the audit obligation.
+  `--skip-green` cannot reuse those gates' publication receipts. Publication
+  holds the destination guard through its check and confirms the target and
+  declarations before recording success. Full runs can discover dependencies
+  omitted from coverage; path declarations themselves do not prove completeness.
+
+SLOTS
+  `[slots]` in `.arc/worktrees.toml` declares a pool of fixed-path checkouts
+  kept across changes, so a change starts from the previous change's build:
+
+    [slots]
+    size = 3                    # 1..=32, fixed once the pool exists
+    root = "~/.worktrees/x"     # default <worktrees_dir>/<repo>-slots
+    documentation = ["*.md"]    # paths ranking ignores (the default)
+    identity = "rustc -vV"      # toolchain identity; its stdout is digested
+    retain = ["target"]         # ignored paths a slot carries between changes
+    invalidate = "<command>"    # discards outputs on an identity change
+    default = true              # begin uses a slot for worktree checkouts
+    warm = "<command>"          # what `arc slots warm` builds a slot with
+    warm_after_integrate = true # integrate starts one warm (default false)
+
+  A pattern without `/` matches a file name at any depth, one with `/`
+  matches from the checkout root, and `*` and `?` stay within one path
+  component. A project without `[slots]` sees no change; a malformed
+  `[slots]` table refuses every command that reads it, as does
+  `warm_after_integrate` without `warm`. The identity command is bounded at
+  120 s and must exit 0; `invalidate` is bounded at 600 s.
+
+  The root holds `pool.json` (the repository's canonical Git common
+  directory and a random pool id), `pool.lock`, and per slot `slot-NN.json`
+  (its state), `slot-NN.use.lock` and the checkout `slot-NN`. A root another
+  repository owns refuses, as does a nonempty directory that is not a pool.
+  The pool lock is innermost: held only around reading and writing slot
+  state, with nothing acquired under it. A slot is reused only when its
+  checkout is registered to this repository at that path with the
+  administrative directory and owner record arc wrote when creating it;
+  unknown ownership refuses reuse.
+
+  `arc begin <slug> --slot`, or any worktree checkout without --worktree,
+  --no-worktree or --adopt when `default = true`, leases the best-ranked free
+  slot under the pool lock and releases the lock. Ranking uses the slot's
+  `built` record (revision and identity digest, set only by a successful
+  preparation or warm in that slot, never by a gate) when its identity matches the
+  identity command's digest in the checkout running begin: known distances
+  first, by paths changed from the built revision to the target outside
+  `documentation`; then built revisions that cannot be compared; then cold
+  slots (no matching build, or no checkout). Ties keep slot order. A slot
+  whose state cannot be read is never handed out, and a free slot still in
+  use is skipped. Exhaustion refuses with the slot table.
+
+  An absent checkout is created with `git worktree add`. A present one must
+  be owned, clean, detached, hold no open change and carry no ignored path
+  outside `retain`, as Git's status lists ignored paths (an emptied outbox
+  is exempt). Otherwise begin refuses naming the slot and the path, and keeps
+  the lease for inspection with the `arc slots recover` command to run once
+  resolved. Begin then runs `git switch -c <branch> <target>` and the
+  identity command in the slot. On a digest change it runs `invalidate` with
+  the gate environment; without one it removes the `retain` paths and the
+  arc-owned storage keyed by the checkout (source-bound builds and receipts,
+  path-stable storage under the Git common directory). Path-stable storage in
+  a project `directory` without `invalidate` refuses the change. Preparation
+  follows. The change records the slot as its worktree, with a `slot-leased`
+  event carrying the lease token. The slot state's phase (`leasing`,
+  `switching`, `recorded`, `warming`, `detaching`, `releasing`) lets
+  `recover` resume an interrupted operation.
+
+  Gates, environment probes and preparation in a slot hold its use lock
+  shared, waiting for an operation that changes the checkout. Begin's
+  switch, warming, integration's target-checkout takeover and merge,
+  integrate's and close's detach, release, recover,
+  `rebase` replay and `rewrite` in a slot checkout hold it exclusively
+  without waiting and refuse while it is held. One arc process holds a slot's lock once; nested
+  work shares that hold. A command in a slot runs under an arc holder that
+  leads its process group and inherits the lock. The holder is a child
+  subreaper: it reports the command's exit and keeps the lock until `wait`
+  reports no child left, so a descendant that detaches or closes its
+  descriptors keeps the slot in use after the arc that launched it is
+  killed. Arc's completion and timeout handling still stop the whole process
+  group, holder included; an escaped descendant then holds the slot only
+  while it keeps the inherited descriptor. Outside slots no holder runs.
+
+  A slot checkout is never removed by `--cleanup` or `[integration] cleanup`.
+  After recording an integration, arc detaches the clean slot at the landed
+  commit, deletes the merged branch with `git branch -d`, records
+  `slot-released` on the change and frees the lease. `close`, whatever its
+  outcome, detaches a clean slot at its `HEAD`, keeps the branch, records
+  `slot-released` and frees it. A slot in use, with uncommitted or untracked
+  work, or failing ownership keeps its lease, and the command prints the
+  release command to run once resolved. A provisional integration resolved
+  by `arc replica provisional resolve` releases its slot the same way, at
+  the accepting integration's commit when this repository holds it and at
+  the slot's `HEAD` otherwise. A slot record naming a pool this repository
+  does not own, as one imported from another replica does, is left alone.
+
+  `arc slots warm [--target <rev>] [--limit <n>]` builds slots before a
+  change needs them. Under the pool lock it ranks the free slots as begin
+  does for the target (default: the primary checkout's branch head) and
+  leases the best one that is not current: current means a `built` record
+  at the target under the identity of the checkout running warm, so a cold
+  slot whose `HEAD` already stands at the target is still warmed. It takes
+  the slot's use lock exclusively for the whole operation, detaches the
+  checkout at the target under begin's reuse rules (creating it when
+  absent, invalidating on an identity change), and runs `warm` under the
+  holder, which receives that exclusive descriptor and never acquires the
+  lock itself. The command runs through the preparation and environment
+  gates use (`ARC_WORKTREE_*`, path-stable storage), so it fills the storage
+  gates consume, at `nice` 19 in the idle I/O class. Success that leaves the
+  checkout detached at the target and reusable records `built` and frees the
+  slot; anything else keeps the lease in phase `warming` and prints the
+  `recover` command, which detaches the clean checkout where it stands and
+  frees it. A running warm's slot is leased, so begin skips it. Warming
+  takes one slot at a time; `--limit` (default 1) bounds how many. Without
+  `warm`, it refuses naming the key.
+
+  With `warm_after_integrate = true`, a successful `arc integrate` starts one
+  detached `arc slots warm --limit 1`, its output in `warm.log` in the pool
+  root, but only when the pool exists and no slot's use lock is held, so no
+  gate or begin of this repository is running in a slot. It never blocks or
+  fails the integration and says what it started or why it started none.
+
+  `arc slots status [--json]` (`arc-slots/1`) shows each slot's phase, lease
+  and token, the change holding the lease with its slug and state (`open`,
+  its closure outcome, `absent`, or `unreadable`; for a lease a landing
+  attempt took to gate in, the attempt with `landing-pending`,
+  `landing-completed` or `landing-abandoned`), checkout `HEAD`, built
+  revision and identity match, rank for the primary checkout's branch, and
+  whether it is in use. Before the pool exists it reports the declared pool
+  as not yet created (`created: false`) and succeeds. A change's slot also appears in
+  `arc status` (`slot`), `arc show` and each `arc stats --round-trip`
+  landing. `arc slots release
+  <slot> --token <t>` frees a lease whose change is closed or absent, or
+  whose landing attempt is finished; both it and `arc slots recover` refuse
+  while the attempt is pending, naming `arc land --recover <attempt>`,
+  which frees the slot itself.
+  `arc slots recover <slot> --token <t>` resumes a phase: a begin whose
+  change exists records the lease on it only when the owned, clean checkout
+  still holds that change's branch at its recorded worktree path, including
+  when the lease event already exists. It takes exclusive use before checking
+  or recording the lease or phase. A begin that opened no change is undone,
+  deleting its branch only while it still points at the target; later phases
+  and warms finish the release. Both refuse an obsolete token, a busy slot,
+  a dirty checkout and unknown ownership. Leases never expire; tokens appear only in
+  status and recovery output. Compiler freshness after a switch stays with
+  the project's build, as for path-stable storage.
 
